WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
It's a new day on SpaceX Daily, Episode 32, coming to you from Vancouver.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.940
It's July 14th, 2026.

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:11.060
Let's get into today's SpaceX developments.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:19.320
B1080's 28th flight targets the 600th Falcon booster reuse, further compressing the marginal

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.260
cost of each kilogram to orbit.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:26.020
Remember, we covered the Falcon reuse cadence on yesterday's episode.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:32.920
Today's flight pushes that further because B1080 is scheduled for its 28th mission on star

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.740
link 10 to 45 from Cape Canaveral.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.320
The flight would mark the 600th time a Falcon 9 first stage has flown again.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.740
The booster has already completed 27 prior flights without structural retirement.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:52.060
This cadence directly tests the current limits of booster life and turnaround processes.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:58.200
The 600th reuse milestone continues the Falcon program's long running effort to drive

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:02.700
launch costs down through repeated recovery and refurbishment cycles.

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:09.540
The star link 10 to 45 mission lifted off from pad 40 at Cape Canaveral Space Force Station

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.160
at 510 AM Eastern Daylight Time.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.320
The Falcon 9 carried 29 star link satellites to low Earth orbit.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:22.620
The first stage performed a boost backburn followed by a landing on the drone ship.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:27.560
This launch added to the ongoing expansion of the broadband constellation.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:33.460
The recovered booster was the same vehicle now approaching its 28th flight, linking the

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:37.040
morning's successful recovery directly to the reuse milestone.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:43.760
A Falcon 9 lifted off from Vandenberg Space Force Base, carrying 27 star link satellites.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:49.640
The mission used a flight-proven first stage that completed its recovery sequence downrange.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.800
The launch occurred on the evening of July 13 local time.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:59.040
It contributed additional capacity to the constellation serving Pacific region users.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:06.260
The Vandenberg flight ran on a separate booster from B1080, illustrating the parallel reuse

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:12.820
streams that now sustain daily star link cadence. The founding flight surgeon for SpaceX is set to

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:19.320
board Soyuz MS-29 for his first space flight. The mission will carry him to the International Space

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:25.700
Station as part of a crew rotation. This marks a personal milestone for a physician who has supported

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:32.500
multiple SpaceX crewed flights from the ground. The flight occurs alongside ongoing preparations for

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:38.180
future Dragon missions. His transition from ground support to orbital crew underscores the

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:44.420
operational continuity between SpaceX's medical oversight and the broader human space flight manifest.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:50.700
NASA selected a Falcon Heavy to launch the Sunrise mission after an earlier vehicle change.

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:56.160
The mission will study solar radio emissions using a constellation of small satellites.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:00.740
The switch aligns the mission with SpaceX's current heavy lift manifest.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:05.440
Integration and launch planning are now proceeding under the new contract.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:11.800
The choice of Falcon Heavy places the science payload on the same heavy lift architecture already

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:18.140
supporting national security and commercial manifests. Remember, we covered the star link expansion

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:23.980
on yesterday's episode. Today's milestone moves that forward because the constellation reached

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:31.340
more than 10,000 satellites in orbit by mid-2026. This figure represents roughly two-thirds of all

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:37.480
active satellites currently in space. The growth stems from repeated Falcon 9 and Starship

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:43.180
deployment flights. The scale now supports expanded direct-to-cell and maritime services.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:49.700
The 10,000 satellite threshold marks a new phase in which constellation density itself becomes the

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:56.060
primary driver of service performance and coverage continuity. Remember, we covered the Starship

00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:02.340
Flight Test campaign on yesterday's episode. Today's development moves that forward because the FAA

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:07.580
closed its review of the Starship Flight 12 mishap and cleared the next test flight.

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:12.960
The approval allows Starship Flight 13 to proceed with planned hardware changes.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:17.660
The agency confirmed that corrective actions meet regulatory requirements.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:25.140
Launch is now targeted for July 16 from Starbase. The clearance resolves the immediate regulatory hold

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:32.040
and returns the program to its iterative flight test cadence. Starlink V3 satellites incorporate

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:38.000
hardware upgrades for direct-to-device connectivity. The new satellites support higher laser link

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:44.420
throughput between units. This architecture expands coverage options for handheld and IOT terminals.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:50.440
Deployment testing begins on the upcoming Starship mission. The V3 designed therefore

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:55.780
bridges the existing broadband constellation with the emerging direct-to-cell service layer.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:02.100
Lab podry observers at Starbase noted increased activity around the launch mount and tank farm

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:08.620
ahead of the next integrated test. Local reports indicate additional propellant loading tests occurred

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:14.280
over the weekend. Community members tracking the site reported visible movement of ground support

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:20.800
equipment near the orbital launch mount. These observations remain unverified by SpaceX but align

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:27.980
with the expected pre-flight flow for flight 13. Reddit users in our SpaceX discuss the FAA's

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:34.020
closure of the flight 12 mishap investigation and what it implies for future return to flight timelines.

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:40.760
Commenters highlighted the speed of the review compared with earlier incidents. Several threads

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:47.100
focused on expected changes to the ship's heat shield and flap actuators. NASA Space Flight published

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:54.460
a weekly launch preview noting seven global missions scheduled including the Soyuz MS-29 crew flight

00:05:54.460 --> 00:06:01.240
and Starship flight 13. The article detailed worldwide range and tracking assets that will support

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:07.620
the Starship test. Readers commented on the overlap between the two missions and potential scheduling

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:13.760
pressure on shared tracking resources. Worth keeping an eye on the concern that reliance on a single

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:21.500
crew vehicle creates a single point of failure for US access to the ISS after 2030. The comments appear

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:27.660
in coverage of long-term crew transportation planning. Any extended grounding of that vehicle would

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:34.640
leave no domestic alternative for crew rotations. The absence of a second certified US crew system

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:40.920
therefore remains the central risk highlighted by the officials. On the AI front, SpaceX and

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:47.640
Cursor released the first jointly developed AI product, an all-round office agent now enclosed beta.

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:54.780
The agent handles document workflows and integrates with existing enterprise tools. Early testers

00:06:54.780 --> 00:07:01.420
describe it as a direct competitor to current chat GPT workplace offerings. The release marks the

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:07.960
initial public output of the SpaceX Cursor collaboration and places rock-derived capabilities inside

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:14.880
enterprise document environments. Pollution from an unpermitted XAI power project is reported

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:20.660
to affect nearby communities disproportionately. The project supplies additional electricity

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:26.060
for Colossus scale training clusters. Local regulators are reviewing compliance

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:31.720
filings tied to the expansion. The episode illustrates the power and citing constraints that a

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:38.800
company rapid growth of the training infrastructure feeding both XAI models and downstream SpaceX adjacent

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:45.840
compute needs. From an engineering standpoint, Falcon 9 first stage reuse centers on a tightly

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:51.400
coupled set of thermal, structural, and propulsion trades that keep the booster within its certified

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:58.560
envelope after each flight. The octagonal landing legs, grid fins, and Merlin 1DVAC engines must

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:04.360
survive repeated entry heating and landing loads while the stages propellant tanks remain leak tight.

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:10.540
Each reuse cycle therefore reduces the marginal cost of the booster to the price of inspection,

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:15.900
refurbishment, and the small amount of additional propellant needed for the recovery burns.

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:23.740
The 600 re-use milestone on B-1080 shows that the cumulative effect of these small

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:29.340
per-flight costs has already driven the effective price per kilogram to orbit well below the

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:35.720
expendable baseline. Continued extension of booster life now hinges on whether the same hardware

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:41.580
can absorb another 10-20 flights before fatigue or thermal degradation forces retirement.

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:47.440
The engineering limit is not thrust or propellant load, but the cumulative damage to the

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:53.420
interstage, raceway covers, and tank domes from repeated entry heating and salt water exposure.

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:59.960
SpaceX's approach has been to accept modest mass penalties for thicker skirts and improved

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:06.240
coatings rather than redesign the entire structure. That choice trades a few kilograms of payload

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:12.160
for dozens of additional flights, collapsing the idiot index, the ratio of finished stage

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:18.640
cost to raw material and energy inputs by roughly an order of magnitude compared with an expendable

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:25.480
vehicle. The next observable data point will be whether B-1080 or its successors can reach

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:32.760
30-35 flights before any structural inspection reveals a hard limit. Starlink V3 satellites now

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:38.800
entering test on Starship Flight 13 introduce higher power laser terminals and direct-to-device

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:45.300
radios on the same bus. These additions increase both intersatellite data rate and ground terminal

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:51.240
flexibility without requiring a full redesign of the satellite structure. The incremental mass

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:57.140
and power budget allocated to the new payloads is offset by the larger fairing volume and higher

00:09:57.140 --> 00:10:03.060
energy margins available on Starship, illustrating how the heavy lift vehicle relaxes constraints

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:09.740
that previously limited constellation upgrades. The immediate engineering question is whether the V3

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:15.500
thermal control and power distribution systems maintain margin through the longer coast and deployment

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:24.060
sequence planned for the upcoming test flight. And a quick market note, SPCX is at $139.14

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:32.540
up 0.9%. Before we go, Starship Flight 13 remains the clearest near-term test of whether the current

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:38.780
hardware iteration closes the gap to rapid reuse. That's the day at SpaceX. That's a wrap.

